    cc_steam_part_reclass: { cell: "K", ev: "neiso-83 (2026-08-05) — `K`, KEEPER. New keeper 2026-08-05-neiso-83-ca1-reclass; control 2026-08-05-neiso-83-control-zerodelta; both registered (rule 15), both --years 2023 2024 2025 in ONE invocation (rule 16), both solved at the same HEAD. PROMOTED ON THE PRE-REGISTERED V3 BRANCH — all six construction properties PASS and none of the four stop-and-escalate triggers fires — on rules 1 [R-STRUCT] / 13 / 14, NOT on a score. Determination CALIBRATED-WITH-CAVEATS in BOTH arms, criterion for criterion identical to the superseded keeper (0 FAILs, 1 ledgered C3c caveat, C1 all 12/12 · free 8/8); C3c bit-unchanged at model 0 >$300 hours all years, no new caveat slot spent; rule 22 D-5(b) re-verification NOT worse. THE CONTROL CAME BACK BYTE-IDENTICAL to the committed keeper (max |class-hour delta| 0.000000 MW, all three years), so unlike neiso-81 there is NO same-HEAD drift to decompose and every arm-B number is the mechanism. THE RESULT IS CORRECT AND NUMERICALLY SMALL AND THAT IS THE CLEAN OUTCOME, not a caveat: largest class move CC_REGULAR +0.0108 TWh in 2025 = 0.018 % of the class (absorbed almost exactly by CT_PEAKER −0.0111), oil −0.00231 / −0.00034 / −0.00139 TWh, λ +0.0005 / −0.0192 / −0.0701 $/MWh, summed C1 |error| delta +0.0044 / −0.0004 / −0.0010 TWh. WHY SO SMALL, MEASURED NOT ASSERTED: 6081's heat rate 10.6062 sits above 97.5 % of NEISO's CC_REGULAR capacity (cap-weighted p50 7.340, p90 8.616; only 312.1 MW dearer), so the re-classed 96 MW is deep out of merit. THE CHARTER'S OWN '< 0.15 % of CC_REGULAR' EXPECTATION WAS CORRECTED BEFORE THE SOLVE, NOT AFTER: Phase 0 measured the block's effective available capacity moving 0.0→0.0 / 61.7→156.4 / 40.5→134.2 MW — more HEADROOM than the charter implied — and the prereg said so in advance; the realized DISPATCH effect then landed an order of magnitude UNDER the charter's bound. DISCLOSED, NOT BURIED: the outage-derate DENOMINATOR moves with the flag (209.1 → 305.1 MW) because it is the same mechanism's own input, and it DOMINATES the headroom (capacity leg +0.0/+28.3/+18.6 MW vs denominator leg +0.0/+66.4/+75.2) — un-forwarded it would remove 221.8 MW for a 152 MW outage. Rule 23 NOT engaged (the deriver's own inputs at 6081 are identical under both arms, so the committed extract re-derives byte-for-byte; nothing regenerated). NEW OPEN ROOT-CAUSE ISSUE FILED, NOT FIXED (rules 19/21/25): campd-unit-outages-NEISO.csv routes plant 6081's DIESEL peakers (units 004/005) to plant_group=CC_REGULAR because `oil` carries no plant_group at all, so in 2024-25 a fully-available CC block is derated to 29.5 % / 19.4 % by outages of machines that are not in it. Benchmark side NOT symmetrically corrected and the residue is measured: the 6081 CA/DFO EIA-923 row is 2,165 MWh in 2023 and ZERO in 2024-25 = 0.0022 TWh, 0.11 % of the C1 band. NEISO population is EXACTLY ONE row: 6081 Stony Brook CA1, 96.0 MW (EIA-860 net summer; 105.0 nameplate — the fleet pmax rule is net-summer-else-nameplate, so the inherited 96-vs-105 mismatch is reconciled, not a discrepancy), carried as `oil` with VOM 4.50 / CO2 1.0 t-MWh / EFORd 0.10 against three CC_REGULAR/gas_cc CT siblings at VOM 2.00 / 0.43 / 0.05 that share its heat rate 10.60617891 exactly. THE EMPTY plant_group IS NOT AN ANOMALY and the inherited framing is corrected: every oil unit in NEISO carries an empty group (135 units / 5,181.5 MW, all empty) because the EIA-860 loader assigns a group only to coal and gas. THE DECISIVE PHYSICS EVIDENCE is measured, not assumed: plant 6081 meters FIVE CEMS units in every train year and NONE is CA1, and EIA-923 Page 1 shows the CA row's own FUEL split tracking its CT siblings' split year by year across a 0.004→0.468 swing in the block's oil share (|gap| 0.0016-0.0217) — the steam part inherits whatever the block burned, so EIA-860's DFO is a duct/legacy label. Steam share 0.2849 ± 0.0060 (2018-22 direct), corroborated for 2024-25 by EIA-923 CT NET exceeding CAMPD CT GROSS (1.343/1.338 — impossible for a CT-only row, so a reporting-convention change, not a retired turbine); the two routes reconcile at a 0.96 auxiliary-load ratio. NONE of it enters the LP — zero fitted parameters. Prereg results/calibration/PREREG-neiso83-stonybrook-ca1-2026-08-05.md (pushed BEFORE either arm solved); FINDING-neiso83-stonybrook-ca1-2026-08-05.md; probes _neiso83_stonybrook_ca1_phase0.py + _neiso83_ca1reclass_ab.py; records _neiso83_stonybrook_ca1_phase0.json + _neiso83_ca1reclass_ab.json. NEISO population is EXACTLY ONE row: 6081 Stony Brook CA1, 96.0 MW (EIA-860 net summer; 105.0 nameplate — the fleet pmax rule is net-summer-else-nameplate, so the inherited 96-vs-105 mismatch is reconciled, not a discrepancy), carried as `oil` with VOM 4.50 / CO2 1.0 t-MWh / EFORd 0.10 against three CC_REGULAR/gas_cc CT siblings at VOM 2.00 / 0.43 / 0.05 that share its heat rate 10.60617891 exactly. THE EMPTY plant_group IS NOT AN ANOMALY and the inherited framing is corrected: every oil unit in NEISO carries an empty group (135 units / 5,181.5 MW, all empty) because the EIA-860 loader assigns a group only to coal and gas. Prereg results/calibration/PREREG-neiso83-stonybrook-ca1-2026-08-05.md; Phase-0 probe scripts/probes/_neiso83_stonybrook_ca1_phase0.py; record _neiso83_stonybrook_ca1_phase0.json." },
