    seam_neighbour_hourly_ladder: { cell: "U", ev: "miso-231 (2026-09-06) — row minted with the field; MISO-scoped so far. The LEVEL-not-SLOPE defect it attacks is generic to any seam ladder held at a FIXED annual price (this ISO included wherever its own ladder is one), but rule 25 [R-ISO-SCOPE] means MISO's verdict never fills this cell: the transfer PRECONDITION is a measured HOURLY neighbour price series for this ISO's own seams under data/raw, which is a DATA question to settle before any charter, and the delta_k offsets must be re-derived from this ISO's own flows and spread. Untested here; nothing licensed." },
    winter_citygate_daily: { cell: "." },
    dual_fuel_switching: { cell: "K", ev: "STAYS K, UNCHANGED -- the row's dual_fuel_switching + dual_fuel_oil_reattribution limbs are untouched and no solve was run. What neiso-110 (2026-09-16, ZERO LP, keeper UNCHANGED) adds is an ev citation this cell never carried, plus the first adjudication of the row's THIRD limb, dual_fuel_oil_daily_parity (shipped default False, False on the keeper). THAT LIMB IS REFUTED AS THE OIL DRIVER, structurally and at zero LP (docs/FINDING-neiso110-winter-oil-driver-2026-09-16.md SS2): (a) byte-identical in 2020 and 2021 -- data/raw/oil-prices/ny_harbor_ulsd_daily.csv carries quotes for 2022-2025 ONLY, so oil_daily_shape_factors returns all-ones and np.array_equal(off,on) holds for both years; (b) it adds ZERO parity-crossing hours in every year and REMOVES 120 in 2025 (OFF->ON: 2020 0->0, 2021 0->0, 2022 48->48, 2023 0->0, 2024 24->24, 2025 792->672); (c) it moves the price the WRONG WAY at Winter Storm Elliott -- the ULSD shape factor on 2022-12-24..27 is 1.0407/1.0407/1.0407/1.0779, i.e. ABOVE 1.0, raising oil parity 20.985 -> 21.839/22.620 against gas of 15.047/15.080/13.570/12.543, because distillate and gas both get dearer in a cold snap. The move Elliott needs is a $5.91/MMBtu LEVEL change (+39.2% on peak gas); the largest downswing the daily shape offers anywhere in 2022 is 22.4%, and on the Elliott days themselves it is an UPSWING. CORRECTION TO THE CHARTER's PREMISE, which named this limb the null on the stated ground that the oil side is 'an ANNUAL constant (resolve_annual_oil_price 18.00/20.64)': it is NOT. dual_fuel.py::dual_fuel_oil_price_series reaches resolve_annual_oil_price only when mode=='forecast'; the keeper is mode=='backcast', where the oil side is the MEASURED MONTHLY EIA-923 Schedule-5 petroleum receipt series (iso_monthly_oil_prices), reported in all 72 months 2020-2025 so the flat OIL_PRICE_PER_MMBTU=18.0 fallback is never reached. The real grain question is monthly-vs-daily, not annual-vs-daily. STILL UNTESTED, AND DELIBERATELY NOT ARMED HERE: whether the daily ULSD shape is worth arming as a rule-14 [R-ACCURATE] accuracy improvement ON ITS OWN MERITS. It is genuinely more accurate than a monthly plateau, but it has no 2020/2021 coverage and its only measurable effect today is to shave 120 hours off the single OVERSHOOT year -- arming it on that basis would be residual-shaving under rule 1 [R-STRUCT]. That question needs its own accuracy-motivated PRECOMMIT and its own screen year; it is a DIFFERENT question from the refutation above and the two are not conflated. WHAT THE CELL NOW ALSO RECORDS ABOUT THE ARMED LIMBS: the keeper's oil is ~98% a LABEL. Splitting the keeper's own oil class by whether the hour is a parity crossing gives, TWh, model oil-fleet vs model switched: 2020 0.0015/0.0000, 2021 0.0212/0.0000, 2022 0.0456/0.0850, 2023 0.0035/0.0000, 2024 0.0033/0.0452, 2025 0.0429/2.3532 -- so 98.2% of 2025 oil is re-attributed switched gas. Against CAMPD actuals the switching channel is ANTI-correlated (corr -0.119; 2022 actual 0.975 vs model 0.085 = 11.5x short, 2025 actual 0.100 vs model 2.353 = 23.5x over). On the twelve largest actual oil-burn days of 2022 delivered gas sat $3.69-8.44/MMBtu BELOW oil parity and the model switched on NONE of them; the two days it does switch (Feb03, Feb14) carry 1.26% of the year's actual oil. The parity test substitutes a PRICE signal for an AVAILABILITY constraint (pipeline non-availability at Elliott), which is why no price-construction fix in this family can close it. NO LEVER PROPOSED and no verdict moves: rule 17 [R-FLOOR-WINDOW] needs a binding window, and the real NEISO oil fleet runs at a 0.3-2.2% capacity factor, so the window has to be derived from the unit record before any floor is named." },
